{"schema_version":3,"catalogue_version":1,"id":"bco:6-1","provision":"BCO 6-1","book":"bco","book_name":"Book of Church Order","abbreviation":"BCO","reference":"6-1","title":"Section 6-1","supplementary":false,"canonical_url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/provisions/bco/6-1/","reader_url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-constitution-reader/#bco/6-1","current_text":{"html":"<p>The children of believers are, through the covenant and by right of birth, non-communing members of the church. Hence they are entitled to Baptism, and to the pastoral oversight, instruction and government of the church, with a view to their embracing Christ and thus possessing personally all benefits of the covenant.</p>","text":"The children of believers are, through the covenant and by right of birth, non-communing members of the church. Hence they are entitled to Baptism, and to the pastoral oversight, instruction and government of the church, with a view to their embracing Christ and thus possessing personally all benefits of the covenant."},"source":{"name":"PCA Constitution Reader","revision":"edce8a8dcd9ced63bb585bf922e6a97319ef62e1","edition":"Current Reader text","files":{"bco.js":"c88e552545b73fd9690de620b237895b7ea52a94bed8e95690c116bf76be8ae5","wcf.js":"1ebf9e54223f7fe4e728a3e640c833a435af825d74b5ef3d4b04687685538a0d","wlc.js":"f6977e761ea4ccc93b07fcacfd0391890cb05b42f4317d807fa293c078d9b051","wsc.js":"dd775417f7ef21e3283bacad5e6ef193c41d64d03bd5a2e8fb934ccca8cb8e53","rao.js":"49808bbb0e95abdc3b07506c87dde0d62d087851e869b49c0f1f2424986450ec"}},"input_fingerprint":"128cf7cdd1263685b0a4c3f3f98d6149b2d89fd2b9b08848b1b45bbc05a5836d","assessment_fingerprint":"517b100d2e9006e313ab6d84d4f0f31593b77832f395383ba2227779470cd034","assessment_summary":{"status":"stale","file":"index/provision_link_adjudications.jsonl","total":11481,"expected":11481,"applied":0,"stale":11481,"unmatched":0,"unassessed":11481},"parent_id":"bco:6","children":[],"coverage":{"relationships":"indexed_records","recommendations":{"status":"incomplete","note":"Recommendation and study papers are not comprehensively indexed by provision."},"history":"partial"},"relationship_count":5,"relationships":[{"id":"bco:6-1--ccb_advice:inquiries/ga50_2023__ci02.md","record_id":"ccb_advice:inquiries/ga50_2023__ci02.md","type":"CCB advice","title":"Chaplain authority to administer sacraments under BCO 8-6","year":2023,"disposition":"in conflict; Adopted 8-0-0","authority_weight":"medium","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/inquiries/ga50_2023__ci02.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/inquiries/ga50_2023__ci02.md","evidence_basis":"structured_provision_tag","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:6-1--ccb_advice:inquiries/ga50_2023__ci02.md:occ-94353c9b0c4a","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/inquiries/ga50_2023__ci02.html","repository_path":"inquiries/ga50_2023__ci02.md","locator":{},"excerpt":"CCB unanimously found Tidewater Presbytery's overture to change BCO 8-6 regarding chaplain administration of sacraments to be in conflict with the Constitution.","sources":[],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/inquiries/ga50_2023__ci02.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null}},{"id":"bco:6-1--case:cases/ga50_2023__2022-20.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga50_2023__2022-20.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Wilson et al. v. Pacific Northwest Presbytery","year":2023,"disposition":"dismissed","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga50_2023__2022-20.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga50_2023__2022-20.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:6-1--case:cases/ga50_2023__2022-20.md:occ-1782eeebd360","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga50_2023__2022-20.html","repository_path":"cases/ga50_2023__2022-20.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":477},"excerpt":"…is clear that “God's people” encompasses non-communicant members (WLC 166: BCO 6-1), those suspended from the Lord's Table (WCF 30:3), associate members (BCO 46-4), etc. However, these parties are not entitled to elect church officers (BCO 20-3; 46-4).…","sources":["case_markdown_text","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current","judicial_cases.jsonl:bco_provisions"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga50_2023__2022-20.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["congregational meeting","minor communicant suffrage","voting rights","communing members","church bylaws","BCO 43-1 justiciability","associate pastor resignation","Covenant Presbyterian Church","Pacific Northwest Presbytery"],"standard_of_review":"not_reached","case_numbers":["2022-20"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"Members challenged a voting restriction imposed at a congregational meeting. The SJC dismissed the complaint as judicially out of order because BCO 43-1 permits complaints against acts or decisions of church courts, and a congregation is not such a court. It identified other possible routes through a report to Presbytery or review of church records. The adopted decision did not decide whether the voting restriction was constitutionally valid."}},{"id":"bco:6-1--case:cases/ga36_2008__2006-02.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga36_2008__2006-02.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Memorial of Central Carolina v. Louisiana","year":2008,"disposition":"other","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga36_2008__2006-02.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga36_2008__2006-02.md","evidence_basis":"structured_case_metadata","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:6-1--case:cases/ga36_2008__2006-02.md:occ-7a171b60d747","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga36_2008__2006-02.html","repository_path":"cases/ga36_2008__2006-02.md","locator":{},"excerpt":"","sources":["cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current","judicial_cases.jsonl:bco_provisions"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga36_2008__2006-02.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["Federal Vision","doctrine of election","covenant theology","water baptism","Westminster Standards","teaching elder examination","memorial procedure","presbytery oversight","erroneous doctrine","TE J. Steven Wilkins","Auburn Avenue theology"],"standard_of_review":"important_delinquency_or_grossly_unconstitutional_proceeding","review_standards":["important_delinquency_or_grossly_unconstitutional_proceeding"],"case_numbers":["2006-2"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"In a BCO 40-5 memorial, Central Carolina Presbytery alleged that Louisiana Presbytery had exonerated TE J. Steven Wilkins despite his published views on election, the church, perseverance, assurance, and baptism allegedly contradicting the Westminster Standards. The SJC sustained the memorial’s gravity and found Presbytery failed to show due diligence, ordering a recorded examination, formal responses, and report. Presbytery later met the procedural directives, but the SJC relied on Case 2007-8’s substantive finding, cited Presbytery under BCO 40-5, and appointed a prosecutor for Case 2007-14."}},{"id":"bco:6-1--overture:ga36_2008:1","record_id":"overture:ga36_2008:1","type":"Overture","title":"Amend BCO 6-1, 6-2, 6-4, and 6-5 to Define Methods of Receiving Church Members","year":2008,"disposition":"Answered in the negative","authority_weight":"low-but-important","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/markdown/ga36_2008.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/markdown/ga36_2008.md","evidence_basis":"title_subject_reference","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id"